WEBVTT

00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.030
Three years ago, AI could finish the line you were typing.

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:08.110
Today, it writes the whole change.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:14.250
The code arrives faster than anyone can review it.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:19.470
And every change still waits for one person.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.490
Factories solved this a long time ago.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.910
They run with the lights off.

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:33.390
Orbi is that line, for software.

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.630
You label a GitHub Issue.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.010
Orbi writes the change.

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:46.610
A separate reviewer checks the work against the Issue, and anything wrong gets fixed before it merges.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:52.210
Only the reviewed commit goes in.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:56.410
And you decide when it ships.

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:02.530
Orbi builds itself this way.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:05.930
Seventy releases in five weeks.

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:10.430
Five hundred of its merged pull requests came off this line.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:16.190
Not ready to hand over the merge? Keep a human gate.

00:01:16.640 --> 00:01:21.250
Orbi stops before the merge, shows what it verified, and waits for your sign-off.

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:25.190
Plug in a better model, and the whole line gets better with it.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.710
Your job moves up the line, to deciding what gets built.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:32.750
File an Issue. Get a release.
